            "HyperText Markup Language, or HTML, and Cascading Style Sheets, or CSS, are two core technologies used to build web pages, but they have completely different jobs. You can think of HTML as the skeleton or the structure of a house. It defines the basic building blocks like headings, paragraphs, lists, images, and buttons on the page. It tells the browser what content is there, but not how it should look.\n\nOn the other hand, CSS is like the paint, furniture, and interior design of that same house. It controls how the HTML elements are presented to the user. With CSS, you can change colors, fonts, spacing, layout, and make the website look great on both a large computer monitor and a small mobile phone screen. \n\nA simple way to see this in action is to imagine a webpage with a button. The HTML creates the button and puts the words click me inside it. By default, it will look like a plain, boring gray box. Then, you use CSS to make that button blue, add rounded corners, change the text color to white, and add a shadow effect when someone moves their mouse over it. Without HTML, there is no content to style. Without CSS, the content is there, but it looks like a plain text document from the early days of the internet.",
